/-

      nockvm-j8check check [--prog NAME] [--coins FILE] [--in DIR] [--dump DIR] [--tamper] [--fast]

  The D2 `CheckMain` contract: exit 0 = ACCEPT, 1 = REJECT, 2 = an input error.  The verdict is `checkJ8`
  (`Nock/V1J8/CheckJ8.lean`, `checkJ8_iff`: under the registry premise, `checkJ8 I = true ↔ ∃ hv, AcceptsJ8V …` at the
  verifier data / message / traces / coins BUILT from the input).

  * `--coins FILE`: the 20 frame coins, 60 unsigned integers (three `𝔽_p` limbs each, coin `χ₀` first), whitespace
    separated; default the example's `χ0`.
  * The input is the example `*[[0 0] [0 1]] = [0 0]` (`Nock/V1J8/Example.lean`), its six chips filled by the module
    fills at the coins (each proved equal to its honest builder, B4750-B4753).
  * `--dump DIR` writes the five present chips' rows (`compute.rows cell.rows walk.rows atom.rows aw.rows`; one line
    per row, the column values as integers in `[0, p)`), before any `--in` (the J chip is absent: zero rows).
  * `--in DIR` replaces each chip whose rows file is present in DIR by the file's rows (row count must equal the
    chip's; missing trailing columns are `0`).  A bad file is an input error (exit 2).
  * `--fast`: the verdict is `checkFast` (= `checkJ8`, `Nock/V1J8/FastCheck.lean`: `checkFast_eq`), compiled with the fast
    `csimp` checkers; same ACCEPT / REJECT, much faster.
  * `--tamper` also runs the example's ten one-change tampers (each must be rejected).
  * `--prog NAME`: the input is GENERATED by the general tracegen `Gen.genJ8` from a run
    of `genX` instead of the example's fixed fills: `example` (`*[[0 0] [0 1]]`, the same input as the default,
    `Gen.genJ8_example`), or a demo of `Nock/TraceGenExe/Demos.lean` (`toy1 toy2 dec add mul fib ack ...`).
    With `--prog`, `--tamper` runs the ten tampers of the generated input.  Without `--coins`, a demo is checked at
    the pseudo-random coins `Gen.prngCoins 1` (`χ0` is degenerate past one row: its walk coin is `1`).
    `mat` is the jetted matmul `n = 2` generated by `Gen.genJ8J` (J chip PRESENT: J rows,
    the `mv` fix, the registry pins, the compiled formula's cells); its `--tamper` adds the seven J tampers
    (`Gen.jTampersOf`: jet result, call output handle, pin serial, pin rank, J publics, the `mv` fix).
    `cnt` / `ne5` / `eq5` are generated by `Gen.genJ8A` (big atoms: the atom table, big
    handles, the cell `kb` columns, the atom walk), subject `p − 1`: `cnt` = `[3 [[4 [4 [0 1]]] [1 0]]]` (the counter
    `p − 1 → p → p + 1`: a conversion row and an inc row; the cell `[p+1 0]`), `ne5` = `[5 [4 [0 1]] [4 [4 [0 1]]]]`
    (op 5 on `p` vs `p + 1`: the atom walk PRESENT), `eq5` = `[5 [4 [4 [0 1]]] [4 [4 [0 1]]]]`; `--tamper` adds
    `Gen.aTampersOf` (limb, inc record, conversion, `mb` / `mi` publics, `kb`, atom-walk records).  On a REJECT the
    eight public equations and the failing source gates of the atom / cell / atom-walk tables are printed.
    `ovf car3 ne7 shs shs2 shc shc2`: MULTI-LIMB atoms (`≥ 2^64`: top-limb overflow, carries
    into suffix rows, first differences deeper than axis 2, shared comb suffixes, a conversion row beside a linked copy
    of `[1, B − 1]`), generated by `Gen.genJ8A` with the big atoms in the SUBJECT.  No accepted claim of feasible
    length reaches them (claim leaves `< p`, `hcen`), so these are judged by the SCOPE-WAIVED harness verdict
    `Gen.checkJ8W` (`checkJ8` minus the claim-scope conjuncts; NOT the verifier): the failing static conjuncts, the
    public equations and the atom rows are printed; `--tamper` adds `Gen.mlTampersOf` (link / overflow rows).

  The coins here are whatever the file says; a true verdict bounds a forgery only when the coins were drawn after the
  traces were fixed (the D2 caveat).
-/
